WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:05.660
What if you could use genetic
engineering to stop humanity's most

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:11.130
dangerous predator, the deadliest animal on the planet responsible for the death

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:17.160
of billions, the mighty mosquito? Along
with other diseases it plays host to

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.189
Malaria, one of the cruelest parasites on Earth

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:23.089
possibly the single biggest killer of
humans in history.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.040
In 2015 alone

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.310
hundreds of millions were infected and almost half a million people died.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:35.480
A new technology could help us eradicate Malaria forever, but to do so we need to

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:37.489
engineer a whole animal population.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:43.130
This is not a hypothetical problem, the
modified mosquitoes already exist in a lab.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.000
Should we use the technology, and is
malaria bad enough to risk it?

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:57.860
(Intro Music)

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.580
Malaria is caused by a group of
microorganisms: Plasmodia, very weird

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:08.510
microorganisms that consists of just a single-cell, they're parasites that

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:13.210
completely rely on mosquitoes. Malaria always starts with an insect bite.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.520
In it's salivary glands, thousands of sporozoites wait until the insect penetrates your

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:23.450
skin, immediately after invading you they head for the liver where they quietly

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:28.460
enter big cells and hide from the immune system. For up to a month they stay here

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.810
in stealth mode consuming the cells
alive and changing into their next form:

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:39.080
small drop like merozoites, they multiply generating thousands of themselves and

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.850
then burst out of the cells. So thousands
of parasites head into the bloodstream

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:48.740
to look for their next victims, Red blood
cells, to stay unnoticed, they wrap

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:53.780
themselves in the membranes of the cells
they killed. Imagine that! Killing someone

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.780
from the inside and then taking their
skin as camouflage, brutal!

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:03.229
They now violently attack red blood cells,
multiplying inside them until they burst

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:07.729
then finding more red blood cells and
this cycle repeats over and over.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.800
Pieces of dead cells spread lots of toxic waste
material, which activates a powerful

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.910
immune response causing flu-like
symptoms, among the symptoms are high

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:21.650
fever, sweats and chills, convulsions,
headaches and sometimes vomiting and

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:26.240
diarrhea. If malaria breaches the
blood-brain barrier it can cause coma,

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.730
neurological damage or death. The
parasites are ready for evacuation now.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:36.400
When another mosquito bites the infected
human they get a ride, the cycle can

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.430
start over.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.060
In 2015, the Zika virus, which
causes horrible birth defects if it

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.410
infects pregnant women, spread rapidly
into new areas around the globe. It too

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:51.960
is carried by a mosquito. The mosquito is the
perfect carrier for human diseases

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.710
they've been around for at least 200
million years. There are trillions of

00:02:55.710 --> 00:03:01.560
them and a single one can lay up to 300
eggs at a time. They are practically

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.510
impossible to eradicate and the perfect
parasite taxi. But today we have a new

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:10.200
revolutionary technology, that could
enable us to finally win the war

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:15.480
against them; CRISPR. For the first time
in human history, we have the tools to

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:20.820
make fast, large-scale changes to entire
species, changing their genetic

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.520
information as we please.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:28.170
So instead of attacking isolated groups
of insects, why not just change the

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.190
types that transmit diseases?

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.180
Using genetic engineering, scientists

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.560
successfully created a strain of
mosquitoes that are immune to the

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:41.010
malaria parasite by adding a new
antibody gene that specifically targets

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:47.400
plasmodium. These mosquitoes will never
spread malaria. But just changing genetic

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.810
information is not enough. The edits
would only be inherited by half the

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:55.470
offspring because most genes have two
versions inside the genome as a

00:03:55.470 --> 00:04:00.060
fail-safe. So after just two generations,
at most only half of the offspring would

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:04.560
carry the engineered gene. In a population
of billions of mosquitoes they would

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.580
hardly make a difference.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.020
A genetic engineering method called the gene drive

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.800
solves this problem.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.340
It forces the new gene to become
dominant in the following generations

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:17.120
overpowering the old gene almost
completely.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.940
Thanks to this twist, 99.5% of all
the engineered mosquitoes offspring will

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:28.440
carry the anti-malaria edit. If we were
to release enough engineered mosquitoes

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.250
into the wild to mate with normal mosquitoes,
the malaria blocking gene would spread

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.370
extremely quickly.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.440
As the new gene becomes a permanent feature of the

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.470
mosquito population, Plasmodium would
lose its home base.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.580
Scientists hope that the change would be
so fast that they could not adapt to it quickly enough.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.360
Malaria could virtually
disappear.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.160
If you take into account that

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:53.070
maybe half a million children are killed
by it every year, about five have died

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:57.600
since this video started. Some scientists
argue that we should use the technology

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.160
sooner, rather than later.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.020
The mosquitoes themselves would probably
only profit from this, they don't have

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.490
anything to gain from carrying parasites
and this might only be the first step

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.680
Malaria might just be the beginning.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.460
Different mosquitoes also carry Dengue

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:18.660
fever and Zika, ticks transmit Lyme
disease, flies transmit sleeping sickness

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.520
fleas transmit the plague. We could save
millions of lives and prevent suffering

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.240
on an unbelievable scale. So, why haven't we
done this yet?

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.360
For one, CRISPR editing is

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.740
barely four years old, so until very
recently we just couldn't do it as fast

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:38.400
and easily. And there are valid concerns.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:42.360
Never before have humans consciously
changed the genetic code of a free-living

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.520
organism on this scale.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:45.780
Once we do it,

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.250
there is no going back. So it has to be
done right, because there could be

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:53.490
unwanted consequences if we set out to
edit nature.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:58.040
In this specific case of malaria though,
the risk might be acceptable

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:01.610
since the genetic modification doesn't
make a big change in the overall genome.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:03.970
It only changes a very specific part.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:06.760
The worst-case scenario here, is probably

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:10.710
that it might not work or that the
parasite adapts in a negative way.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:13.340
There is still much debate.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:17.500
Technology as powerful as gene drive, needs to be

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:22.520
handled with a lot of care but at some
point we have to ask ourselves: Is it

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:28.130
unethical to not use this technology,
when every day 1,000 children die.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:32.020
Humanity has to decide how to act on
this in the next few years.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.160
The public discussion is way behind the technology
in this case.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:38.180
What do you think?

